+**Goal.** Motion that is correct for things that move on their own. This is the
+stage that makes frame generation actually good, and the one that unlocks running
+neural rendering and frame generation together.
+
+**How.** Intercept vertex shader creation (`init_pipeline` / `create_pipeline`),
+and make the shader also output the previous frame's clip-space position.
+
+Two routes:
+
+**(a) Automated bytecode patching.** Parse DXBC, add an output register, append
+instructions that compute the previous clip position from a previous world matrix
+supplied through an extra constant buffer slot. This is what an automated
+solution does, and it covers every shader without curation. It needs a DXBC
+assembler. Note that the well-known open-source one is GPL-3, which is
+incompatible with this project's MIT licence — a clean-room implementation would
+be required, not a copy.
+
+**(b) Targeted replacement through RenoDX.** You are already building inside the
+RenoDX tree, and shader dumping plus hash-keyed replacement is precisely what
+that framework does. Dump the scene vertex shaders, identify the ones that
+actually matter, hand-write HLSL replacements that also emit motion, compile with
+DXC, swap by hash at pipeline creation.
+
+**Route (b) is the pragmatic one**, because you do not need per-object motion for
+*everything*. Static world geometry is already exact from Stage 2. What needs
+per-object motion is the set of things that move independently — vehicles,
+pedestrians, animated props. That is tens of shaders, not hundreds, and the
+classification data tells you which ones they are.
+
+**The genuinely hard part is not the injection.** It is object identity across
+frames: to compute previous clip position you need the object's *previous*
+transform, which means recognising that this draw call is the same object as one
+from the last frame. Candidate keys are a hash of index buffer plus vertex buffer
+plus draw offsets, or the contents of the object's own constant buffer. Skinned
+characters need previous bone matrices as well, which multiplies the state you
+have to carry.
+
+Expect this stage to be where most of the total effort goes.
+
+**Ships.** Ghost-free frame generation. `GeometryMotion` reaches `READY`, and the
+shared-contract flag becomes truthful, so neural rendering and frame generation
+run together — the arbitration path that is already implemented and tested.
+
+**Effort.** Months.
+
+---
+
+## Stage 6 — Upscaling
+
+**Goal.** Render below native resolution, reconstruct to native.
+
+**How.** Intercept scene-sized render target creation and scale it; adjust
+viewports and scissors to match; inject a per-frame jitter into the projection
+matrix before the constant buffer is uploaded (Halton, and hand the same offsets
+to DLSS); evaluate DLSS-SR on low-resolution colour plus depth plus motion;
+composite the interface on top at full resolution.
+
+**Why this is last.** It is the most invasive stage by a wide margin. Post-process
+chains that assume a fixed relationship between buffer sizes break. Interface
+elements that were full-resolution now are not. Anything that samples a
+scene-sized target with hard-coded texel offsets misbehaves. Every one of those is
+found by playing, not by reading code.
+
+**Worth asking before starting it.** Upscaling pays off when you are GPU-bound.
+GTA V on a populated RP server is frequently CPU-bound instead, and no amount of
+upscaling helps there. Measure your actual bottleneck on the servers you play
+before committing months to this stage.
+
+**Effort.** Months.
+
+---
+
+## Total, honestly
+
+Stages 1–3 are weeks of work and deliver real improvements to neural rendering on
+their own. Stages 4–6 are the bulk, and a realistic estimate for a solo developer
+is measured in quarters, not weeks. The existing paid solution in this space
+represents years of work; there is no version of this that is a weekend.
+
+The sequencing above is designed so you can stop after any stage and still be
+ahead of where you started.
+
+---
+
+## The thing to settle before any of it
+
+Everything from Stage 2 onwards means patching the game's shaders and running
+ReShade with full add-on support. That is exactly the class of client
+modification that FiveM's Pure Mode and server anticheat exist to block, and a
+server that forbids it will keep forbidding it no matter how good the
+implementation is.
+
+So the question to answer first is not technical:
+
+> On the servers I actually play, is a full add-on ReShade stack with patched
+> shaders permitted?
+
+If the answer is no, Stages 4–6 produce something you cannot use where you want to
+use it. If the answer is yes for some servers, the work is worth doing, and the
+status model already handles "installed correctly but not permitted here" as a
+distinct state rather than as a failure.
+
+---
+
+## Licensing note
+
+Reimplementing a technique from its public description is fine. Copying code,
+binaries, shader patches or authentication from a paid mod is not, and this
+project has been explicit about that from v2 onwards. Keep it that way — the
+architecture documentation says so, and it should stay true.
+
+---
+
+## Suggested first move
+
+Stage 1 and the detector for it. It is days of work, it has an unambiguous pass
+condition (does the reconstructed camera position track the player?), and every
+later stage is blocked on it.
+
+The second move is the flag split described in Stage 2, so that camera-only motion
+can light up the neural path without ever being mistaken for something the
+generator is allowed to use.
+
+---
+
+# Becoming the provider
+
+Everything above was written on the assumption that somebody else runs the
+model and we work out what they are doing. `core/backend.hpp` turns that
+assumption into a variable.
+
+## Why this is the pivot
+
+Look at what is currently built and inert:
+
+| Built, tested, switched off | Blocked on |
+|---|---|
+| The 1.00–2.00 second-pass dial | two feature handles with separate histories |
+| Per-material weights inside the model | a control mask reaching the evaluation |
+| The antialiasing benefit at render scale 1.00 | driving the projection jitter |
+| Rendering below native | the same, plus the game's own TAA |
+| Generated frames | the D3D11/D3D12 interop and Reflex |
+
+That is five different features, and it looks like five different problems. It
+is one problem: **the feature is created and owned by somebody else, so its
+inputs are not ours to supply.** Every row above follows from that single fact,
+and every row above is fixed by the same single change.
+
+So the question is not "can we add these five things". It is "do we own the
+feature". Owning it is Stages 4 to 6, and those stages were always the large
+part of the work — the interop, the jitter, disabling the game's TAA. What the
+backend interface changes is that they no longer have to land all at once: a
+native backend can exist alongside the third-party one and be chosen only for
+what it has actually proven.
+
+## The rule that does not relax
+
+A capability of a backend we wrote may be **declared** — the statement and the
+implementation are in the same repository and change together. A capability of
+anyone else's backend must be **observed**, every session, because their next
+release can remove it and will not tell us.
+
+Those are different functions in `core/backend.hpp` and the distinction is
+asserted in `test_backend.cpp`. Collapsing them into one `supports` flag would
+be the convenient thing to do and would quietly undo the entire evidence model —
+a stale flag is exactly how a feature ends up switched on and doing nothing,
+which is the v2 bug this rewrite exists to fix.
+
+`NativeTarget()` is the specification the native path has to meet, written in
+code rather than in prose so it is compiled and asserted. A test checks it is
+strictly better than what the third-party path can be observed to do — if it
+were not, there would be no reason to write it — and that it never loses a
+capability the existing path has. A replacement that drops something is not a
+replacement.
+
+## Order of work
+
+1. **Stage 3, unchanged.** HUD-less colour, the interface layer, draw
+   classification. This is also where the semantic mask comes from, so it now
+   pays for two features rather than one. Nothing else moves until it is done.
+2. **A native backend that does nothing but the image pass.** NGX directly, one
+   feature handle, no generation, no upscaling — render scale 1.00, which is
+   where the personal setup already sits. Correctness first, on the smallest
+   possible surface.
+3. **Jitter and the game's TAA.** The antialiasing benefit at 1.00 becomes real,
+   and reconstruction below 1.00 becomes possible for the first time.
+4. **The second handle.** The two-pass dial stops being inert. So does the
+   control mask, which makes the per-material weights exact rather than
+   composited.
+5. **Stage 4 as written: interop, DLSS-G, Reflex.** The largest single piece,
+   and deliberately last, because it is the one that can destabilise everything
+   before it.
+
+## What not to claim
+
+Until step 2 exists, this is a control layer with a well-specified interface for
+becoming something else. The interface is real, compiled and tested. The native
+backend is not written. Those are different things and the status vocabulary
+already has words for the difference.
+
+---
+
+# Stage 3, part one: telling the frame apart
+
+Built. `core/frame/draw.hpp` and `core/frame/segmentation.hpp`, with the draw
+observer that feeds them in the input provider.
+
+## The rule
+
+Interface is drawn last. Not "usually last" — last, because compositing order is
+what makes it visible, and a renderer that drew the HUD before the world would
+be drawing a HUD nobody can see. So the frame ends in a run of interface draws,
+and the capture point is where that run begins.
+
+That gives a rule which recognises no interface element at all: find the last
+draw that is definitely world or image work, and cut after it. Text, icons,
+bars, markers, and a browser-rendered layer the game does not own all land on
+the far side of the cut without being identified individually. On a server with
+a large custom interface, that is the difference between a rule that works and a
+list somebody has to maintain.
+
+## Why nothing here returns a bare answer
+
+Being wrong is expensive in both directions. Cut too early and the world's last
+effects are treated as interface, which freezes part of the picture. Cut too
+late and text is smeared by the pass that was meant to improve it. Neither is
+subtle, and both are reported as "it looks broken" rather than as a setting.
+
+So `ClassifyDraw` returns a *prior*, with a confidence and a reason, and it is
+deliberately denied any knowledge of where it is in the frame. A full-screen
+quad with blending is either a browser layer or a bloom composite; only the
+frame knows which, so the per-draw rule says so instead of guessing. Keeping
+that split is what stops a per-draw rule from being tuned until it fits one
+scene and breaks on the next.
+
+The segmenter then requires the boundary to hold still for 30 frames before it
+is used, and drops it after 6 that disagree. Losing it is faster than finding it
+on purpose: every frame spent using a stale boundary is a frame drawn wrongly. A
+resolution change invalidates it outright rather than waiting to be noticed, and
+a near-empty frame — a loading screen, a device reset — is ignored rather than
+believed, because admitting one as evidence is how the boundary ends up at zero
+and stays there.
+
+## What this earns, and what it does not
+
+`CapId::FrameSegmentation` is a new node, and `HudlessColor` now requires it.
+Knowing *where* to capture and *having captured* are different claims, so they
+are different bits and different nodes. This build earns the first. The second
+is still masked off in the provider, alongside everything else that is not yet
+true.
+
+That distinction is the whole reason the segmentation work can ship before the
+capture work without anything in the interface becoming a lie.
+
+# Stage 3, part two: the copy
+
+Built. `HudlessCapture` in the input provider.
+
+This is the first code in the product that does something to the frame rather
+than watching it, so it is arranged to be as close to inert as a thing that
+allocates a texture can be:
+
+- **Only behind a locked boundary.** Without the lock there is no capture and
+  the product behaves exactly as it did before this existed.
+- **Copied immediately before the first interface draw.** ReShade's draw event
+  fires *before* the draw, so copying "after the last world draw" means copying
+  at the draw that follows it — which has to be exactly the first interface
+  draw. That off-by-one is the whole correctness of the feature, so it is
+  asserted in `test_frame_segmentation.cpp` rather than left to be discovered.
+- **No resolve, no conversion, no scaling.** The destination is the source's own
+  description with the heap and usage changed, so there is no format decision to
+  get wrong. A multisampled target would need a resolve, which is a different
+  operation with different failure modes, so it is refused with a reason rather
+  than done quietly.
+- **One failure switches it off for the session.** A copy that failed once will
+  fail sixty times a second, and the second failure carries no information the
+  first did not.
+- **Barriers are issued unconditionally.** D3D11 ignores them; D3D12 does not,
+  and getting them wrong there is a corrupted copy rather than an error.
+
+## What it earns
+
+`kProviderHudless` is no longer masked off — but it is a claim about the last
+few frames, not about the session. A copy that worked once and has since stopped
+is not a HUD-less image, and reporting it as one would be precisely the class of
